{"name":"Gilded Pesto","hex":"#bbaf5e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gilded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of gild"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the color or quality of gold.","Made of gold or covered by a thin layer of gold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gilded"},{"term":"pesto","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sauce, especially for pasta, originating from the Genoa region in Italy, made from basil, garlic, pine nuts, olive oil and cheese (usually pecorino)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pesto"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gilding","blocks":[{"text":"Gilding is a decorative technique for applying a very thin coating of gold over solid surfaces such as metal, wood, porcelain, or stone. A gilded object is also described as \"gilt\". Where metal is gilded, the metal below was traditionally silver in the West, to make silver-gilt objects, but gilt-bronze is commonly used in China, and also called ormolu if it is Western. Methods of gilding include hand application and gluing, typically of gold leaf, chemical gilding, and electroplating, the last also called gold plating. Parcel-gilt objects are only gilded over part of their surfaces. This may mean that all of the inside, and none of the outside, of a chalice or similar vessel is gilded, or that patterns or images are made up by using a combination of gilt and ungilted areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg/330px-Gilded_frame_being_burnished_with_agate_tool.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilding","wikidata":"Q1334300"},{"title":"Pesto","blocks":[{"text":"Pesto, also known as pesto genovese, is an Italian paste traditionally made with leaves of Genovese basil, extra virgin olive oil, Parmesan, pecorino sardo, pine nuts, and garlic. It originated in the Ligurian city of Genoa and is used to dress pasta."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/BasilPesto.JPG/330px-BasilPesto.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pesto","wikidata":"Q9896"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg/500px-Belt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Belt Buckle with nomadic-inspired zoomorphic design, manufactured in China for the Xiongnu. Mercury-gilded bronze (a Chinese technique invented by the Daoists in the 4th century CE). North China, 3rd–2nd century BC.[4][5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABelt_Buckle_with_Zoomorphic_Design%2C_North_China%2C_3rd-2nd_century_BCE.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg/500px-Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg/1280px-Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg/500px-Gilding_Rock_Cen_Prometheus_jeh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Regilding the statue Prometheus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.or